  "evidence": "A: rebuild a fully public NHS; free personal care and £20bn a year for social care; immediate anti‑poverty measures (+£40/week to Universal Credit, scrap two‑child cap, uplift disability and carers’ benefits); rent controls and 150,000 social homes a year; 10:1 pay ratios, wealth tax on assets over £10m, align capital gains with earnings; cancel Trident, reform NATO toward de‑escalation and no‑first‑use, end Prevent; strong animal and nature measures (phase out factory farming, ban blood sports, Rights of Nature). B: right to see a GP in 7 days with 8,000 more GPs, urgent NHS dentistry, mental health hubs in schools, a cancer treatment guarantee; free personal care, a Carer’s Minimum Wage and paid carer’s leave; expand free school meals to children in poverty; apprenticeships, Lifelong Skills Grants, support for entrepreneurs and small firms, replace business rates with a Commercial Landowner Levy, manage public finances responsibly, flexible merit‑based work visas; restore community policing with all burglaries attended and tackle court backlogs; maintain NATO and the nuclear deterrent, reverse Army cuts and move toward 2.5% of GDP on defence; strong but measured climate and nature plans.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly emphasize Care, but B couples it with proportionality cues (support for entrepreneurs, merit‑based visas, and fiscal responsibility) and clearer loyalty/authority appeals (robust defence posture and community policing), matching the electorate’s next‑highest priorities. A leans more heavily into equality and sanctity (sweeping redistribution and nature/animal purity) while downplaying loyalty and authority (cancel Trident, reform NATO, end Prevent), diverging from the profile’s balance.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "B"
